{{ layout "layouts/appLayout.vto"}}
<style>
  @import url("https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap");

  :root {
    --ink: #111318;
    --muted: #5f656f;
    --line: #e2e6ec;
    --accent: #15566c;
    --accent-soft: #e4f0f3;
    --paper: #f7f9fb;
  }

  * {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  body {
    min-height: 100vh;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: "Space Grotesk", "Trebuchet MS", sans-serif;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, #ffffff 0%, #f7f9fb 78%, #e4e8ee 100%);
  }

  #main-content {
    padding: 28px 20px 46px;
  }

  .page {
    max-width: 980px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .nav {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 6px 0 18px;
    gap: 14px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .brand {
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 1rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .nav-links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .nav a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .nav a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(280px, 0.86fr);
    gap: 30px;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 34px 0 24px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.15em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    line-height: 1;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .hero p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .hero-media {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .hero-media img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .hero-media figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .post {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .section {
    padding: 26px 0 0;
  }

  .section h2 {
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    font-size: 1.22rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .section p {
    margin: 0 0 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.72;
  }

  .section strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .feature-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 10px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .feature-list li {
    padding: 12px 13px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: #ffffff;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .callout {
    margin-top: 16px;
    padding: 15px 16px;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
  }

  .callout p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  footer {
    margin-top: 32px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-top: 14px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 20px;
    }

  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    #main-content {
      padding: 20px 16px 38px;
    }

    .hero {
      padding-top: 26px;
    }

    .feature-list {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>

<div class="page">
  {{ include "partials/nav.vto" }}

  <header class="hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Paragliding build</p>
      <h1>soloTow</h1>
      <p>
        A small pay-in tow winch I built for flatland paragliding. The goal was simple: make towing less dependent on
        extra vehicles, extra people, and expensive equipment, without ignoring the places where a remote winch can get
        dangerous.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-media">
      <img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/brian-greeson/soloTow/main/guide/IMG_1869.jpeg"
        alt="soloTow winch build in the field">
      <figcaption>soloTow field build with the tow drum, frame, and drive hardware assembled.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </header>

  <main class="post">
    <section class="section">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Flying in the flatlands requires towing. Unfortunately, towing usually adds a lot of logistics: a dedicated tow
        vehicle, extra people, and an expensive payout tow winch.
      </p>
      <p>
        soloTow is a DIY attempt to make towing more portable and accessible. It is not automatic, beginner-proof, or a
        commercial tow system. The goal is a portable winch that gives a current, capable pilot more independence when
        the site and weather make sense.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>What mattered</h2>
      <p>
        Making something pull a line is not the interesting part. The useful version had to be buildable without a
        machine shop, easy to inspect in the field, and conservative about what happens when communication gets flaky or
        something stops behaving.
      </p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Aluminum extrusion for the frame, with very few custom parts.</li>
        <li>3D printed pieces for small mounts and packaging problems.</li>
        <li>Off-the-shelf drive, battery, controller, and radio hardware.</li>
        <li>Controller power limiting so the winch is not trying to overpower the pilot.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Why not pay out towing?</h2>
      <p>
        soloTow is a pay-in towing system. The pilot launches away from the winch and flies toward the line as it is
        pulled in. That choice matters because some failures are more forgiving. If the winch or controller quits doing
        useful work, line tension tends to fall off instead of staying loaded.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          That does not make towing safe. It just means a bad day at the winch is more likely to become a normal landing
          than an ongoing pull. For this project, that was a better default than a payout setup.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>System architecture</h2>
      <p>
        The drive side is a <strong>5 kW hub motor</strong> controlled by a <strong>Kelly motor controller</strong>,
        mounted into an aluminum extrusion frame with the tow drum and line. The remote side uses
        <strong>Heltec LoRa remote</strong> hardware to send commands from the pilot back to the winch.
      </p>
      <p>
        I kept the layout deliberately plain. You can look at it and understand what each part is doing. The frame is
        not hiding behind custom fabrication, and the printed parts solve small problems instead of becoming the whole
        project.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Remote safety</h2>
      <p>
        The remote has a <strong>deadman switch</strong>. The controller also times out if it stops hearing from the
        remote, so the winch is not allowed to keep acting on stale commands. Tuning that timeout was less obvious than
        it sounds.
      </p>
      <p>
        At launch, the pilot can be low to the ground and about 1 km away. That is exactly when the RF link is weakest,
        and it is also one of the worst times to lose command authority. Clothing or a harness could block the signal.
        The timeout had to be short enough to fail safe, but not so short that the winch cut out constantly during
        setup. The <strong>Controller power limit</strong> is another simple guardrail: keep normal operation away from
        aggressive over-tow forces in the first place.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>What I would improve next</h2>
      <p>
        The radio link is the first thing I would improve. A relay radio station placed closer to the pilot would keep a
        cleaner link between the pilot and winch, especially when the pilot is low, far away, or partially blocked by
        gear.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other obvious improvement is weight. The current motor works, but a smaller winch motor would make the whole
        system easier to move without changing what I actually need this winch to do.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Field validation</h2>
      <p>
        The current system is fully functional. It has completed <strong>60 successful tows</strong>, with an average tow
        altitude around <strong>650 ft</strong>. Those flights are the part that matters most to me. They exposed the
        real setup time, the real radio behavior, and the real tow loads better than bench testing ever could.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Safety note</h2>
      <p>
        Paragliding and towing are dangerous. This is not for inexperienced pilots, club or commercial towing, or anyone
        trying to skip proper instruction and judgment.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    Built and field tested by Brian Greeson.
  </footer>
</div>

{{ /layout }}
